function renderGreReview(){
 if(lang==='en')return `<button class="projects-back" data-open="notes">← Back to Notes</button>
 <p class="story-eyebrow">June 18 · Study Notes</p><h2 id="story-title" tabindex="-1">${greReviewTitle.en}</h2>
 <p class="story-lead">I just finished taking the GRE on June 18, and while everything is still fresh in my mind, I thought I'd share a little reflection on my month of preparation.</p>
 <p>First, a bit about my background. I'm currently a junior at a Sino-foreign joint university, and I scored a perfect score on the new TOEFL this May without any preparation. So I would say I have a relatively solid foundation in English.</p>
 <p>Starting in February, I had been studying vocabulary on and off, working through ZW's 3,000-word list and synonym groups twice using the vocabulary app <em>Bu Bei Dan Ci</em>. I also practiced a few questions here and there. However, this approach didn't seem particularly effective. I kept forgetting words almost as quickly as I learned them.</p>
 <p>On May 19, I finally decided to get serious and purchased a one-month BBGRE premium membership. Even then, I wouldn't describe my preparation as particularly intensive. Between my internship and my own tendency to procrastinate, I usually had only one or two hours a day to dedicate to the GRE.</p>
 <p>With that limited time, I first worked through the curated question banks for Text Completion and Reading Comprehension. After finishing those, I started taking one practice test each day, while using BBGRE's vocabulary tools to reinforce words commonly tested in Sentence Equivalence questions. For the Quantitative section, I completed the questions in the highly curated question bank and spent extra time reviewing topics I wasn't completely comfortable with, particularly permutations and combinations.</p>
 <p>One of my biggest takeaways from this month of preparation is that <strong>the GRE involves a surprising amount of luck.</strong></p>
 <p>Because of how questions are selected, there's a real possibility of encountering questions you've already practiced. In fact, during my exam, I felt that I had seen roughly one-third of the questions before.</p>
 <p>On the other hand, once you reach the harder section, you may also encounter questions that leave you second-guessing yourself, especially longer reading passages and logical reasoning questions where none of the answers seems entirely convincing.</p>
 <p>For particularly tricky Reading Comprehension questions, my strategy is to begin by understanding the overall argument of the passage, especially its subject and central claim. Then, I identify exactly what the question is asking about before returning to the passage to locate relevant evidence and make inferences.</p>
 <p>For logical reasoning questions, I highly recommend drawing out the logical structure of the argument. Identifying the premises, conclusions, and connections between them makes the reasoning much clearer. I also found the logical reasoning videos from Qingshan Academy particularly helpful.</p>
 <p>Finally, I want to share a few words of encouragement for anyone currently preparing for the GRE.</p>
 <p>When I first started practicing, I found the exam quite intimidating. With so little time available each day, I kept telling myself that I could always retake it if things didn't work out.</p>
 <p>But after about fifteen days of consistent practice, I began to realize that the vocabulary and reasoning patterns tested on the GRE are actually quite repetitive. The night before my exam, I was still encountering plenty of words I had never seen before. Yet that didn't stop me from achieving a score I was happy with.</p>
 <p>Looking back, I think <strong>developing a feel for the questions matters more than simply maximizing the number of words memorized or problems completed.</strong> Understanding how the exam works, recognizing recurring patterns, and learning to trust your reasoning can make a real difference.</p>
 <p>And perhaps most importantly, get enough sleep the night before, walk into the exam with confidence, and trust the work you've already put in.</p>`;
 return `<button class="projects-back" data-open="notes">← 返回笔记</button>
 <p class="story-eyebrow">备考笔记</p><h2 id="story-title" tabindex="-1">${greReviewTitle.zh}</h2>
 <p class="story-lead">6 月 18 日刚考完 GRE，趁记忆还比较新鲜，来总结一下这一个月备考吧。</p>
 <p>先说一下我的基本情况：中外合办大三在读，今年五月份裸考新托福满分，所以自认为英语基础还是相对不错的。从 2 月份我就开始断断续续背 zw 的 3000 词和等价词，在「不背单词」上过了两遍，同时也零星刷了一些题目。但是感觉这样的效率并不高，单词也是前背后忘。</p>
 <p>从 5 月 19 日开始，终于下定决心充了 BBGRE 的大会员一个月，开始认真刷题。说是认真也远远不算高强度，因为实习的原因（加上自己比较懒），大部分时间每天都只有 1–2 小时留给 GRE。在这有限的时间里，我先是刷完了精选题库的填空和阅读题，然后开始每天一套模考，同时用 BBGRE 的背单词功能巩固六选二的单词。数学部分由于时间有限，我只是刷完了超级精选题库的所有题目，并重点过了一下不太熟练的题型，比如排列组合题。</p>
 <p>这一个月以来刷题最大的感受就是，GRE 其实是一个比较看运气的考试。一方面，由于它抽题的机制，很有可能你会在考场上遇到之前做过的原题（我在考场上感觉有三分之一的题都做过）。但另一方面，当你进入 hard 部分，也可能遇到一些让你反复纠结、无法说服自己的题目，尤其一些中长文章的阅读和逻辑题。</p>
 <p>对于较为“刁钻”的阅读题，我个人的经验就是先找到文章的大意，尤其是 subject 和主要论点，接下来再去重点关注题目的 subject，最后才是回到文章去找信息、做推断。对于逻辑题，我推荐大家画出逻辑链条，把前提和结论具体是什么搞清楚，也可以过一遍青山学堂的逻辑题视频，讲得非常清晰。</p>
 <p>最后一段想写一下对正在备考的大家的鼓励。刚开始刷题的时候，我也怀着极大的畏难心理，加上每天学习的时间非常有限，所以一直消极地想大不了就再来一次。可是当坚持了十五天左右后，我就发现实际上 GRE 常考的单词和题目思路都是有限的；考前的一天晚上回顾生词时，甚至还有很多“第一次”见到的单词，但这并不影响我在正式考试中获得理想的成绩。所以，我会说比起刷很多题背很多单词，更重要的是去培养自己做题的感觉，考前一天保持充足的睡眠，然后自信地完成考试。</p>`;
}
function renderIstanbul(){
 return `<button class="projects-back" data-open="notes">${choose('← Back to Notes','← 返回笔记')}</button>
 <p class="story-eyebrow">${choose('Travel Notes','旅行笔记')}</p><h2 id="story-title" tabindex="-1">${istanbulTitle[lang]}</h2>
 ${choose(
 `<p>One afternoon in the distant days of online classes, I opened what looked like a book of photographs. It read more like an ethnography. Hundreds of old photographs traced how the wave of modernization in the early twentieth century reshaped this thousand-year-old city and the lives of the many communities that called it home. What stayed with me were the crumbling walls that seemed to be everywhere. In black and white, the buildings that had held so much laughter and so many tears stood in silence, their edges still sharp even as they gave way under the weight of history. The Turkish people, suddenly faced with a new world order, seemed unsure how to find their footing. And yet the author wrote, “Why can’t we leave this place? Because I can’t imagine what life would be like if I didn’t live in Istanbul.” From then on, Istanbul, or Constantinople, was more than a name in a history book. It was a place I longed to visit, touched by mystery and melancholy.</p>
 <p>The Istanbul I saw was different from the city of a century ago I had imagined through Pamuk’s writing. On the European side, colorful apartment buildings lined the cobbled lanes, with flowers spilling from the windows. On the Asian side, I caught more glimpses of local life. Fewer people were calling out to customers, and even the seagulls seemed to take their time. On İstiklal Avenue, the souvenir shops and chain stores were what caught my eye, much like those in any tourist district. Still, there was a beauty here that went beyond the buildings and the times that had shaped them.</p>
 <p>I felt the warmth of the city from the first day, when someone pressed a transit card into our hands as we stood hesitating in front of a metro ticket machine. When the power went out in our apartment in the old city on the European side, our host waited by the door until nearly midnight for us to come back. He went looking for flashlights and candles and stayed until the power came back on.</p>
 <p>On our last afternoon, we were wandering down bustling İstiklal Avenue when we stepped into a music shop. The owner was teaching a Chinese woman to play a hand drum. When he saw us, he picked up a traditional Turkish instrument and joined her in an impromptu duet. We burst into applause. He served us tea and invited us to play along. We lost track of time and forgot the world outside. Two hours went by as we tried the handpan, hand drum, guitar, and ukulele. We had no language in common, yet through music we could understand each other.</p>
 <p>Outside, we passed more little music shops, their narrow, dim interiors hung with instruments from around the world. I imagined that each one had an owner who loved music just as fiercely. An instrument is lucky to end up where it belongs. For a shop to hold on to its place on İstiklal Avenue takes the persistence of the person behind the counter.</p>
 <p>As a visitor, I caught only glimpses of what it is like to live in Istanbul. Like anywhere else, life here has its share of disappointments. Even so, I want to believe the city will be kind to those who meet it with gratitude, and that its kind, resilient people will write the next chapter of its history.</p>`,
 `<p>在遥远的网课时代，某天下午，我翻开了一本摄影集，或者准确来说是一本民族志。它用数百张老照片记录了二十世纪初期现代化浪潮冲击下，伊斯坦布尔这座千年城市的风貌，以及其中各种族居民的生活。印象最深的是照片里随处可见的断井残垣。在黑白照里，曾经承载着那么多欢笑和眼泪的建筑物们，只是默默地棱角分明，被历史压得不堪重负而土崩瓦解，而刚刚面对新世界秩序的土耳其人，则茫然而无所适从。然而，作者总结道：“为什么我们不能够离开此地？那是因为我无法想象，不生活在伊斯坦布尔，会是什么模样。”从那时候开始，伊斯坦布尔或君士坦丁堡对我而言就不再只是一个历史书上的名词，而是一个带着几分神秘、几分哀愁的向往之地。</p>
 <p>今天我见到的伊斯坦布尔已经和帕慕克笔下百年前的城市不尽相同。欧洲区的石板路小巷两边是五彩斑斓的公寓，花团锦簇缀满窗户，而亚洲区则有着更多本地人的生活气息，少有人忙着招揽顾客，海鸥也开始闲庭信步。独立大街两侧最显眼的，是和其他城市和景区别无二致的纪念品店和连锁品牌。可是我依然觉得这座城市有它独特的美，这种美丽超越了它的建筑与所处的时代。</p>
 <p>从第一天在地铁站自动售票机前徘徊时被塞来一张交通卡开始，我们就感受到了这座城市的温暖。欧洲老城区的公寓停电时，房东将近凌晨还一直站在门口等着我们回来，并为我们四处寻找可以照明的手电和蜡烛，一直等到电力恢复后才安心离去。</p>
 <p>最后一天下午，我们在熙熙攘攘的独立大街闲逛，走进一家乐器店，正好看到老板在教授一位中国姐姐手鼓，看到我们来了，老板拿起手中的土耳其传统乐器，为我们即兴合奏一曲，在我们鼓掌惊叹之后，他用茶水款待我们，让我们也来一起玩音乐。于是我们在这里忘记了时间也忘记了外面的世界，不知不觉地度过了两个小时，尝试了各种各样不同的乐器，手碟、手鼓、吉他、尤克里里。哪怕语言不通，音乐的力量让我们可以感受到彼此的心灵相通。</p>
 <p>走出这家小店，又看见许多小乐器店，窄小而昏暗的门面里琳琅满目地挂着各国乐器，我想，它们一定也有一个坚定地爱着音乐的主人。适得其所是作为乐器的幸福，在独立大街上有一席之地则离不开店主的坚持。</p>
 <p>作为游客我无从窥见伊斯坦布尔居民生活的全貌，也知道和任何其他城市的居民一样，生活不总是幸福美满，一定有许多不如意的时刻。可是还是愿意相信这座城市会善待心存感恩之心的人们，而这些善良而坚韧的人们，会书写它历史的新篇章。</p>`
 )}`;
}
